<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lapland (Finland)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Finnish Lapland, Lappi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lapland is the northernmost region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, covering nearly a third of the country's area but home to fewer than 200,000 people, with Rovaniemi as its capital. Long inhabited by the Sámi, it is a land of forest, fells and tundra largely north of the Arctic Circle. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lapland-war">Lapland War</a> of 1944–1945 retreating German troops burned Rovaniemi and much of the province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lapland-finland.jpg" alt="Lapland (Finland)" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Romano Ando · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arctic_circle_santa_village.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lapland is the largest and northernmost region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, with an area of 100,367 square kilometres. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and its borders follow the rivers Tana, Muonio and Torne. Its landscape runs from vast mires and forests in the south to fells in the north, and the highest point in Finland, 1,324 metres on Halti, lies on its border. The Arctic Circle crosses the region, so the midnight sun, the polar night and the northern lights can be seen there. Winters last about seven months. The lower course of the Tornio was the last part of Finland to be deglaciated, about 10,100 years ago.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1634 to 1809 Lapland was split between two counties of the Swedish Realm: the northern and western areas belonged to Västerbotten County, and the southern areas to Ostrobothnia County. In 1809 the northern and western areas were transferred to Oulu County, and Lapland Province was separated from Oulu Province in 1938.</p><p>During the Interim Peace and the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continuation-war">Continuation War</a> the Finnish government allowed the German army to station itself in Lapland. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> made a separate peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1944, the Soviet Union demanded that Finland expel the Germans. In the resulting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lapland-war">Lapland War</a> almost the whole civilian population was evacuated, and the Germans used scorched earth tactics before withdrawing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. Between 40 and 47 per cent of the dwellings in Lapland were destroyed, along with 417 kilometres of railway and 675 bridges, and ninety per cent of Rovaniemi was burned to the ground.</p><p>After the war the municipality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petsamo">Petsamo</a> and part of Salla were ceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and the following decades brought rebuilding, industrialisation and fast economic growth, with large hydroelectric plants and mines. In the late 20th century the population began to decline in most of the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lapland has 21 municipalities, which cooperate in a regional council, and Rovaniemi is its regional centre. It is by far the most sparsely populated part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, with about 3 per cent of the country's people. The Sámi homeland, formed by the northernmost municipalities, lies within it, and Northern Sámi, Inari Sámi and Skolt Sámi are spoken there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lapland (Finland)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
